  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead didn't hold. That's the only result that matters.",
    "what_this_means": "They took a 2\u20130 lead in the first inning and held it through five. Then Seattle scored twice in the fourth to tie it, twice more in the seventh to break it, and the Giants never answered. Robbie Ray pitched four solid innings before exiting, then Adrian Houser gave up three runs in three innings. The actual damage. Bryce Eldridge hit a solo homer and drove in a run, and Heliot Ramos added an RBI, but five hits and sixteen strikeouts is not an offense. The game turned in the sixth inning and stayed gone. They had this game. They couldn't keep it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Two runners left in scoring position. They converted one of those chances into a run. Not nothing, but not enough when the pitching falters after the starter leaves. Ray's exit created a gap. Houser couldn't hold it. The 3\u20136 loss drops them back half a game in the division and makes it four losses in five days. This is inconsistency in its clearest form: they showed enough to lead, not enough to finish. One clean inning from the bullpen changes everything. They didn't get it. Offense that doesn't drive multiple runners doesn't survive ragged relief in a close game.",
